is Play W4. So maybe the current discard is B7 from the previous player? Wait, the previous player's last action was Play B8, so the current discard is B7 from someone else. Maybe the player across or the next player? It's a bit confusing. But according to the valid actions, Chi B7 is available, so the current discard is B7 from the player to my left. \n\nAssuming that, I can try to form a chow with B7. Since I have B6 and B7 in my hand, if the discarded tile is B5, I could form B5-B6-B7. But the discarded tile is B7, so the possible chow would be B5-B6-B7. But I need to have B5 in my hand. Wait, no. If the discarded tile is B7, then the chow would be B5, B6, B7. But I have B6 and B7 in my hand. So if I have B5, I could form the chow. But I don't have B5 in my hand. Therefore, I can't form a chow with B7. Wait, maybe I'm misunderstanding the Chi notation. The note says that Chi B7 refers to the completed chow sequence. So if the discarded tile is B7, and I have B5 and B6, then I can form B5-B6-B7. But I don't have B5. Alternatively, if the discarded tile is B7, and I have B6 and B7, but need B5. Since I don't have B5, I can't form the chow. Therefore, the Chi B7 is not possible? But why is it a valid action? Maybe I have another tile that can form a chow. Let me check again.\n\nWait, my hand has B3, B6, B7. If the discarded tile is B7, maybe the chow is B3-B4-B5? No, because the discarded tile is B7. Or B6-B7-B8. I have B6 and B7, but the discarded tile is B7. So to form B6-B7-B8, I need a B8. Do I have B8 in my hand? No, but the unshown tiles have B8:3. Wait, but the player across has played B8. So if the discarded tile is B7, and I have B6 and B7, I can form B6-B7-B8 by taking the B7 and having B8 in my hand. But I don't have B8. So I can't form that chow. Therefore, the Chi B7 action is invalid? But it's listed as a valid action. There must be something wrong with my analysis.\n\nWait, maybe the Chi B7 is for a different sequence. Let me think again. The notation says that Chi X refers to the completed chow. So if the action is Chi B7, the completed chow is B5-B6-B7. To form this, I need to have B5 and B6 in my hand, and take the B7 from the discard. Do I have B5 in my hand? No. So I can't form that. Alternatively, maybe the chow is B6-B7-B8. Then I need B6 and B8, and take B7. But I don't have B8. So again, no. What about B4-B5-B6? I have B6, but not B4 or B5. So no. The only possible chow with B7 is B5-B6-B7 or B6-B7-B8, but neither is possible because I don't have the necessary tiles. Therefore, the Chi B7 action is not actually possible. But it's listed as a valid action. Maybe I made a mistake in the hand analysis.\n\nWait, looking at my hand again: B3 (2), B6 (1), B7 (1). So I have two B3s, one B6, one B7. If the discarded tile is B7, maybe the chow is B3-B4-B5. But I don't have B4 or B5. Or B4-B5-B6, but no B4 or B5. Or B5-B6-B7, but no B5. Or B6-B7-B8, no B8. So none of these. Therefore, the Chi B7 is invalid. But why is it a valid action? Maybe there's a mistake in the game state. Alternatively, maybe I have another tile that I missed. Let me check again. My hand tiles are [W2:2; W7:1; W8:1; T8:1; T9:1; B3:2; B6:1; B7:1; F1:1; F2:1; J1:1]. So B3 is two, B6 and B7 are one each. So no B5. So Chi B7 is not possible. Therefore, the correct action is to Pass.\n\nBut the valid actions include Chi B7. Maybe there's a misunderstanding. Let me re-examine the Chi notation. The note says that the notation 'Chi X' refers to the completed chow sequence. So if the action is Chi B7, the completed chow is [B5, B6, B7]. The discarded tile could be B5, B6, or B7. If the discarded tile is B7, then I need B5 and B6. If I have those, I can form the chow. But I don't have B5. So no. Therefore, the Chi B7 is invalid. Hence, the correct action is to Pass.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The valid action Chi B7 is not feasible because the necessary tile B5 is missing to form the chow [B5, B6, B7]. \nReason: To form a chow with B7, the hand must