o others and allows me to keep the most flexibility.\n\nIf I discard W5, which is a single tile not part of any meld, and there are three left, that might be safe. Also, if I keep the other tiles for possible melds. Alternatively, discarding F2 (South Wind) might be safe since my seat wind is North, and the prevailing is East. Others might not be focusing on South Wind pungs. The next player has played F1 (East), so maybe they are going for a Four Winds hand. If I discard F2, they might take it. But the next player's melds are empty, so maybe not. The previous player has a B4 kong, so maybe they're not focused on winds.\n\nAnother thought: if I discard a tile that could complete a chow for others. For example, if I discard W8, and someone has W7 and W9, they could chi W8. But I have W7 and W8, so if I discard W8, someone might take it. However, since I have W8, maybe keeping it is better.\n\nGiven all this, perhaps the best move is to discard W5. It's a middle tile not part of any current meld in my hand, and there are still three left in the unshown. This minimizes the chance of helping others while keeping my own hand flexible for future melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding W5 is optimal as it minimizes aiding opponents while maintaining flexibility for future melds.]\nReason: [W5 is a middle tile not part of any existing meld in my hand. With three unshown W5s remaining, discarding it reduces the chance of others completing chows or pungs. Keeping other tiles like W2 (two copies), B3 (two copies), or honor tiles preserves potential for concealed pungs, chows, or dragon-based scoring. Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) remains viable, and avoiding high-value discard (e.g., J1) prevents opponents from forming dragon pungs.]\nAnswer: Play W5"}
