, it's less likely. Alternatively, playing B7, which has 2 left. \n\nBut the unshown B3 has 4, B7 has 2. So maybe B7 is more likely to be needed. \n\nAlternatively, maybe playing W2 is better. Since I have two, and it's not part of a Chow, maybe it's safer. \n\nWait, the previous player has a Pung of W1, but that's different from W2. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best move is to play B3, as it's part of a pair and not part of any Chow. That way, I can keep the T1-6 for Chows. \n\nSo the answer would be Play B3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for a Fully Concealed Hand and All Chows. Playing B3 or B7 could help maintain concealed melds while reducing risk.\nReason: B3 is part of a pair and not part of any Chow sequences, making it a safer discard. Playing B3 preserves the potential for Chows in the T1-T6 sequence and maintains concealed status.\nAnswer: Play B3"}
